"OWL 2 Web Ontology Language Direct Semantics (Second Edition) (https://www.w3.org/TR/owl2-direct-semantics/)\nciteturn160view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.w3.org/TR/owl2-direct-semantics/\",\"lineno\":162}); Total lines: 361\nL129:   * ⋅ ^{OP} is the object property interpretation function that assigns to each object property OP ∈ V_{OP} a subset (OP)^{OP} ⊆ Δ_{I} × Δ_{I} such that\nL130:     * (owl:topObjectProperty)^{OP} = Δ_{I} × Δ_{I} and\nL131:     * (owl:bottomObjectProperty)^{OP} = ∅.\nL132:   * ⋅ ^{DP} is the data property interpretation function that assigns to each data property DP ∈ V_{DP} a subset (DP)^{DP} ⊆ Δ_{I} × Δ_{D} such that\nL133:     * (owl:topDataProperty)^{DP} = Δ_{I} × Δ_{D} and\nL134:     * (owl:bottomDataProperty)^{DP} = ∅.\nL135:   * ⋅ ^{I} is the individual interpretation function that assigns to each individual a ∈ V_{I} an element (a)^{I} ∈ Δ_{I}.\nL136:   * ⋅ ^{DT} is the datatype interpretation function that assigns to each datatype DT ∈ V_{DT} a subset (DT)^{DT} ⊆ Δ_{D} such that\nL137:     * ⋅ ^{DT} is the same as in D for each datatype DT ∈ N_{DT}, and\nL138:     * (rdfs:Literal)^{DT} = Δ_{D}.\nL139:   * ⋅ ^{LT} is the literal interpretation function that is defined as (lt)^{LT} = ( LV , DT )^{LS} for each lt ∈ V_{LT}, where LV is the lexical form of lt and DT is the datatype of lt.\nL140:   * ⋅ ^{FA} is the facet interpretation function that is defined as ( F , lt )^{FA} = ( F , (lt)^{LT} )^{FS} for each ( F , lt ) ∈ V_{FA}.\nL141:   * NAMED is a subset of Δ_{I} such that (a)^{I} ∈ NAMED for each named individual a ∈ V_{I}.\nL142: The following sections define the extensions of ⋅ ^{OP}, ⋅ ^{DT}, and ⋅ ^{C} to object property expressions, data ranges, and class expressions.\nL143: #### 2.2.1 Object Property Expressions\nL144: \nL145: The object property interpretation function ⋅ ^{OP} is extended to object property expressions as shown in Table 1.\nL146: \nL147: Table 1. Interpreting Object Property Expressions\nL148: Object Property Expression  | Interpretation ⋅ ^{OP}\nL149: --- | ---\nL150: ObjectInverseOf( OP )  | { ( x , y ) | ( y , x ) ∈ (OP)^{OP} }\nL151: #### 2.2.2 Data Ranges\nL152: The datatype interpretation function ⋅ ^{DT} is extended to data ranges as shown in Table 3. All datatypes in OWL 2 are unary, so each datatype DT is interpreted as a unary relation over Δ_{D} — that is, as a set (DT)^{DT} ⊆ Δ_{D}. OWL 2 currently does not define data ranges of arity more than one; however, by allowing for n-ary data ranges, the syntax of OWL 2 provides a \"hook\" allowing implementations to introduce extensions such as comparisons and arithmetic.\nL153: An n-ary data range DR is interpreted as an n-ary relation (DR)^{DT} over Δ_{D} — that is, as a set (DT)^{DT} ⊆ (Δ_{D})^{n}\nL154: Table 3. Interpreting Data Ranges\nL155: Data Range  | Interpretation ⋅ ^{DT}\nL156: --- | ---\nL157: DataIntersectionOf( DR_{1} ... DR_{n} )  | (DR_{1})^{DT} ∩ ... ∩ (DR_{n})^{DT}\nL158: DataUnionOf( DR_{1} ... DR_{n} )  | (DR_{1})^{DT} ∪ ... ∪ (DR_{n})^{DT}\nL159: DataComplementOf( DR )  | (Δ_{D})^{n} \\ (DR)^{DT} where n is the arity of DR\nL160: DataOneOf( lt_{1} ... lt_{n} )  | { (lt_{1})^{LT} , ... , (lt_{n})^{LT} }\nL161: DatatypeRestriction( DT F_{1} lt_{1} ... F_{n} lt_{n} )  | (DT)^{DT} ∩ ( F_{1} , lt_{1} )^{FA} ∩ ... ∩ ( F_{n} , lt_{n} )^{FA}\nL162: #### 2.2.3 Class Expressions\nL163: \nL164: The class interpretation function ⋅ ^{C} is extended to class expressions as shown in Table 4. For S a set, #S denotes the number of elements in S.\nL165: Table 4. Interpreting Class Expressions\nL166: Class Expression  | Interpretation ⋅ ^{C}\nL167: --- | ---\nL168: ObjectIntersectionOf( CE_{1} ... CE_{n} )  | (CE_{1})^{C} ∩ ... ∩ (CE_{n})^{C}\nL169: ObjectUnionOf( CE_{1} ... CE_{n} )  | (CE_{1})^{C} ∪ ... ∪ (CE_{n})^{C}\nL170: ObjectComplementOf( CE )  | Δ_{I} \\ (CE)^{C}\nL171: ObjectOneOf( a_{1} ... a_{n} )  | { (a_{1})^{I} , ... , (a_{n})^{I} }\nL172: ObjectSomeValuesFrom( OPE CE )  | { x | ∃ y : ( x, y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} and y ∈ (CE)^{C} }\nL173: ObjectAllValuesFrom( OPE CE )  | { x | ∀ y : ( x, y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} implies y ∈ (CE)^{C} }\nL174: ObjectHasValue( OPE a )  | { x | ( x , (a)^{I} ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} }\nL175: ObjectHasSelf( OPE )  | { x | ( x , x ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} }\nL176: ObjectMinCardinality( n OPE )  | { x | #{ y | ( x , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} } ≥ n }\nL177: ObjectMaxCardinality( n OPE )  | { x | #{ y | ( x , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} } ≤ n }\nL178: ObjectExactCardinality( n OPE )  | { x | #{ y | ( x , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} } = n }\nL179: ObjectMinCardinality( n OPE CE )  | { x | #{ y | ( x , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} and y ∈ (CE)^{C} } ≥ n }\nL180: ObjectMaxCardinality( n OPE CE )  | { x | #{ y | ( x , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} and y ∈ (CE)^{C} } ≤ n }\nL181: ObjectExactCardinality( n OPE CE )  | { x | #{ y | ( x , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} and y ∈ (CE)^{C} } = n }\nL182: DataSomeValuesFrom( DPE_{1} ... DPE_{n} DR )  | { x | ∃ y_{1}, ... , y_{n} : ( x , y_{k} ) ∈ (DPE_{k})^{DP} for each 1 ≤ k ≤ n and ( y_{1} , ... , y_{n} ) ∈ (DR)^{DT} }\nL183: DataAllValuesFrom( DPE_{1} ... DPE_{n} DR )  | { x | ∀ y_{1}, ... , y_{n} : ( x , y_{k} ) ∈ (DPE_{k})^{DP} for each 1 ≤ k ≤ n imply ( y_{1} , ... , y_{n} ) ∈ (DR)^{DT} }\nL184: DataHasValue( DPE lt )  | { x | ( x , (lt)^{LT} ) ∈ (DPE)^{DP} }\nL185: DataMinCardinality( n DPE )  | { x | #{ y | ( x , y ) ∈ (DPE)^{DP}} ≥ n }\nL186: DataMaxCardinality( n DPE )  | { x | #{ y | ( x , y ) ∈ (DPE)^{DP} } ≤ n }\nL187: DataExactCardinality( n DPE )  | { x | #{ y | ( x , y ) ∈ (DPE)^{DP} } = n }\nL188: DataMinCardinality( n DPE DR )  | { x | #{ y | ( x , y ) ∈ (DPE)^{DP} and y ∈ (DR)^{DT} } ≥ n }\nL189: DataMaxCardinality( n DPE DR )  | { x | #{ y | ( x , y ) ∈ (DPE)^{DP} and y ∈ (DR)^{DT} } ≤ n }\nL190: DataExactCardinality( n DPE DR )  | { x | #{ y | ( x , y ) ∈ (DPE)^{DP} and y ∈ (DR)^{DT} } = n }\nL191: ### 2.3 Satisfaction in an Interpretation\nL192: \nL193: An axiom or an ontology is satisfied in an interpretation I = ( Δ_{I} , Δ_{D} , ⋅ ^{C} , ⋅ ^{OP} , ⋅ ^{DP} , ⋅ ^{I} , ⋅ ^{DT} , ⋅ ^{LT} , ⋅ ^{FA} , NAMED ) if the appropriate condition from the following sections holds.\nL194: #### 2.3.1 Class Expression Axioms\nL195: \nL196: Satisfaction of OWL 2 class expression axioms in I is defined as shown in Table 5.\nL197: Table 5. Satisfaction of Class Expression Axioms in an Interpretation\nL198: Axiom  | Condition\nL199: --- | ---\nL200: SubClassOf( CE_{1} CE_{2} )  | (CE_{1})^{C} ⊆ (CE_{2})^{C}\nL201: EquivalentClasses( CE_{1} ... CE_{n} )  | (CE_{j})^{C} = (CE_{k})^{C} for each 1 ≤ j ≤ n and each 1 ≤ k ≤ n\nL202: DisjointClasses( CE_{1} ... CE_{n} )  | (CE_{j})^{C} ∩ (CE_{k})^{C} = ∅ for each 1 ≤ j ≤ n and each 1 ≤ k ≤ n such that j ≠ k\nL203: DisjointUnion( C CE_{1} ... CE_{n} )  | (C)^{C} = (CE_{1})^{C} ∪ ... ∪ (CE_{n})^{C} and\nL204: (CE_{j})^{C} ∩ (CE_{k})^{C} = ∅ for each 1 ≤ j ≤ n and each 1 ≤ k ≤ n such that j ≠ k\nL205: #### 2.3.2 Object Property Expression Axioms\nL206: \nL207: Satisfaction of OWL 2 object property expression axioms in I is defined as shown in Table 6.\nL208: Table 6. Satisfaction of Object Property Expression Axioms in an Interpretation\nL209: Axiom  | Condition\nL210: --- | ---\nL211: SubObjectPropertyOf( OPE_{1} OPE_{2} )  | (OPE_{1})^{OP} ⊆ (OPE_{2})^{OP}\nL212: SubObjectPropertyOf( ObjectPropertyChain( OPE_{1} ... OPE_{n} ) OPE )  | ∀ y_{0} , ... , y_{n} : ( y_{0} , y_{1} ) ∈ (OPE_{1})^{OP} and ... and ( y_{n-1} , y_{n} ) ∈ (OPE_{n})^{OP} imply ( y_{0} , y_{n} ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP}\nL213: EquivalentObjectProperties( OPE_{1} ... OPE_{n} )  | (OPE_{j})^{OP} = (OPE_{k})^{OP} for each 1 ≤ j ≤ n and each 1 ≤ k ≤ n\nL214: DisjointObjectProperties( OPE_{1} ... OPE_{n} )  | (OPE_{j})^{OP} ∩ (OPE_{k})^{OP} = ∅ for each 1 ≤ j ≤ n and each 1 ≤ k ≤ n such that j ≠ k\nL215: ObjectPropertyDomain( OPE CE )  | ∀ x , y : ( x , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} implies x ∈ (CE)^{C}\nL216: ObjectPropertyRange( OPE CE )  | ∀ x , y : ( x , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} implies y ∈ (CE)^{C}\nL217: InverseObjectProperties( OPE_{1} OPE_{2} )  | (OPE_{1})^{OP} = { ( x , y ) | ( y , x ) ∈ (OPE_{2})^{OP} }\nL218: FunctionalObjectProperty( OPE )  | ∀ x , y_{1} , y_{2} : ( x , y_{1} ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} and ( x , y_{2} ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} imply y_{1} = y_{2}\nL219: InverseFunctionalObjectProperty( OPE )  | ∀ x_{1} , x_{2} , y : ( x_{1} , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} and ( x_{2} , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} imply x_{1} = x_{2}\nL220: ReflexiveObjectProperty( OPE )  | ∀ x : x ∈ Δ_{I} implies ( x , x ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP}\nL221: IrreflexiveObjectProperty( OPE )  | ∀ x : x ∈ Δ_{I} implies ( x , x ) ∉ (OPE)^{OP}\nL222: SymmetricObjectProperty( OPE )  | ∀ x , y : ( x , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} implies ( y , x ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP}\nL223: AsymmetricObjectProperty( OPE )  | ∀ x , y : ( x , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} implies ( y , x ) ∉ (OPE)^{OP}\nL224: TransitiveObjectProperty( OPE )  | ∀ x , y , z : ( x , y ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} and ( y , z ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP} imply ( x , z ) ∈ (OPE)^{OP}\nL225: #### 2.3.3 Data Property Expression Axioms\nL226: \nL227: Satisfaction of OWL 2 data property expression axioms in I is defined as shown in Table 7.\nL228: Table 7. Satisfaction of Data Property Expression Axioms in an Interpretation\nL229: Axiom  | Condition\nL230: --- | ---\nL231: SubDataPropertyOf( DPE_{1} DPE_{2} )  | (DPE_{1})^{DP} ⊆ (DPE_{2})^{DP}\nL232: EquivalentDataProperties( DPE_{1} ... DPE_{n} )  | (DPE_{j})^{DP} = (DPE_{k})^{DP} for each 1 ≤ j ≤ n and each 1 ≤ k ≤ n\nL233: DisjointDataProperties( DPE_{1} ... DPE_{n} )  | (DPE_{j})^{DP} ∩ (DPE_{k})^{DP} = ∅ for each 1 ≤ j ≤ n and each 1 ≤ k ≤ n such that j ≠ k\nL234: DataPropertyDomain( DPE CE )  | ∀ x , y : ( x , y ) ∈ (DPE)^{DP} implies x ∈ (CE)^{C}\nL235: DataPropertyRange( DPE DR )  | ∀ x , y : ( x , y ) ∈ (DPE)^{DP} implies y ∈ (DR)^{DT}\nL236: FunctionalDataProperty( DPE )  | ∀ x , y_{1} , y_{2} : ( x , y_{1} ) ∈ (DPE)^{DP} and ( x , y_{2} ) ∈ (DPE)^{DP} imply y_{1} = y_{2}\nL237: #### 2.3.4 Datatype Definitions\nL238: \nL239: Satisfaction of datatype definitions in I is defined as shown in Table 8.\nL240: \n"
